The cheetah's roar is not as powerful and deep as a lion's roar; rather, it is more similar to shouted words expressing irritation.
The cheetah prefers to eat small animals. It hunts monkeys, snakes, rats, and if possible, birds too. The animals that the cheetah hunts are considerably smaller than itself.
Scientists believe that wild cheetahs track their prey by scent and follow the smell to the place where animals live.
They can see well and move quietly forward in the dark using night vision. The savanna cheetah blends in with its surroundings and launches attacks on its prey.
When a small group of organisms (a small population) becomes separated from a larger population (for example, if they move across a mountain range or a river, or if they migrate to a new island), and cannot easily return, they often find themselves in a different environment than the one they previously inhabited.
In a new environment, there are diverse resources and various competitors. Therefore, populations will require a wider range of features or adaptations, compared to what they previously needed, in order to compete vigorously.
There has been absolutely no change in the original population; they still require the same adaptation as before.
As time passes, as the new settlement gradually adapts to its new environment, it begins to resemble other settlements more and more.
Eventually, thousands or perhaps millions of years later, these two populations will look so different that they can no longer be considered the same species.
We call this process "speciation," which means the formation of new species. Speciation is an inevitable outcome and a very important part of evolution.
