The Great Pyramid of Giza is the only wonder of the Seven Wonders that still stands in its place today.
The Great Pyramid, built by the Egyptians in the third century BCE, is one of several large pyramids constructed in honor of deceased pharaohs.
The Giza Plateau, also known as the "Giza Necropolis," located in the Valley of the Dead in Egypt, includes numerous pyramids (the Great Pyramid being the largest), several smaller tombs, various temples, and the Great Sphinx.
The Great Pyramid was built in honor of the Pharaoh Khufu, and many smaller pyramids, tombs, and temples were constructed in honor of Khufu's wives and members of his family.
