The theory of technological determinism is a term that practically encompasses a wide range of ideas—from the notion that technological progress or the inevitability of modern technology to the strong belief that human destiny is governed by an underlying logic rooted in scientific laws and their manifestation in technology.
Most interpretations of technological determinism share two common ideas: first, that technological development follows a path that is independent of cultural or political influences, and second, that technology in turn exerts effects on societies that are inherited rather than socially conditioned.
For example, someone could say that a motorcar necessarily smoothens the way for road development.
However, it is not economically viable to lay down a nationwide road network just for a few cars, so new production methods have been introduced to reduce the cost of car ownership.
Large-scale car ownership also leads to a high rate of accidents on roads, prompting the development of new techniques in the healthcare sector to repair damaged bodies.
A major element in Romanticism is cultural determination, derived from writers such as Goethe, Gottlieb Fichte, and Schiller.
In the context of romanticism, geography shaped individuals, and over time, traditions and cultures associated with that geography—those that were in harmony with the society's environment—proved to be more effective than rigid, universally applicable laws.
Just as Paris is known today as a center of fashion, Constantinople was regarded as the center of fashion in feudal Europe.
It is known to have begun around 400 AD as a center of pleasure and luxury, and it remained in existence until the 11th century AD.
The main reason for its decline in the 12th century was that the Crusaders returned home carrying silks and spices as gifts, which were worth more than the prices offered in Byzantine markets.
This was the time when the title of Fashion Capital shifted from Constantinople to Paris.
The Gothic style flourished between the 10th and 11th centuries and the 14th century.
Clothing was initially greatly influenced by Byzantine culture in the East.
However, due to the slow communication system, the West could lag behind by 25 to 30 years.
In the late Middle Ages, Western Europe began to develop its own style. One of the most significant advancements at the time was the popularization of the practice of using buttons to fasten the clothing of Crusaders.
Subsistence farming is a type of farming carried out only to produce food sufficient to meet the needs of the farmer and his family.
Sustainable agriculture is simple, often organic, and achieves maximum yield by using locally adapted, protected seeds in conjunction with crop rotation or other relatively simple techniques, thus working in harmony with the environmental system.
Historically, most farmers have been engaged in agriculture for subsistence, and this situation still prevails in many developing countries.
Subcultures bring together like-minded individuals who feel alienated by mainstream social standards and provide them with a legitimate means to develop a sense of identity.
Subcultures can be specific to certain groups based on age, race, class, location, and/or gender.
The characteristics that distinguish a subculture may include a combination of linguistic, aesthetic, religious, political, sexual, geographical, or other factors.
Members of subcultures often signal their membership through distinctive and symbolic styles, such as clothing fashion, unique mannerisms, and speech patterns.
One of the most common ways used to highlight social significance is identifying, during their upbringing, a few unpleasant incidents in children's lives where they were neglected by adults, deliberately or accidentally subjected to abuse, and prevented from participating in social activities.
Children like these are called "feral" or wild. Some feral children are confined by people (usually their parents); in some cases, parents distance themselves from the child because they reject the child's severe mental or physical disability.
The wild children must have endured severe abuse or trauma before being left behind or running away.
Others are said to have been raised by animals; some are reported to have been living in the jungle in caves.
When raised completely by non-human animals, wild children (within psychological limits) exhibit behaviors similar to those of fully reared animals, such as showing fear of humans or indifference toward them.
