Many people have observed that the synchrony in physiology and behavior often depends on the presence of circadian rhythms and biological clocks.
Such rhythmic patterns, which are not merely responses to external rhythmic stimuli, have been described in most living organisms, including bacteria, fungi, plants, and animals.
The biological clock is an oscillating system that will continue to maintain its free-running period even in the absence of external cues.
The Hershey-Chase experiment was the most important in identifying DNA as the genetic material.
Hershey and Chase used bacteria, or viruses, to insert their DNA into a bacterium.
They conducted two experiments: one to produce DNA from phage using radioactive phosphorus, and another to produce protein from phage using radioactive sulfur.
Depending on the type, the significance of the affected genetic material, and whether the affected cells are malignant, the alteration can produce various effects.
Only alterations occurring in germ line cells can be passed on to children, while mutations in any other location may cause cell death or cancer.
Ecotourism attracts individuals interested in visiting natural areas, seeking to enjoy scenic views, including wildlife such as plants and animals.
Examples of activities available at the site include hunting, fishing, photography, bird watching, park walks, and studying information about the ecosystem.
An example of this is visiting, photographing, and learning about the orangutans in Borneo.
Every morning, people leave small towns across the country by car for their workplaces, leaving behind those whose workplaces are the very places they have just departed from.
In this kinetic transport shuttle, everyone is connected to one another, and it is helping the private car-based transportation system.
Science today indicates that this carbon-based economy has displaced one of the stable conditions that have supported human evolution for the past two million years.
Everyone in the society uses the transportation system. Yet, it is strange that nearly everyone has a complaint about the transportation system.
In developed countries, you occasionally hear complaints about water quality or pipe bursts, just like these.
Why do transportation systems generate such complaints, and why do they fail on a daily basis? Are transportation engineers simply incompetent, or is there something inherently flawed about the system itself?
Traffic flow refers to the study of individual drivers, the movement of vehicles between two locations, and their interactions with one another.
Unfortunately, studying traffic flow is very difficult because driver behavior cannot be predicted with 100% certainty.
Fortunately, drivers tend to behave within appropriate and consistent boundaries; thus, there is a certain degree of stability in traffic flows, which can to some extent be described in a mathematical manner.
Relationships have been established among three fundamental aspects to better manage traffic flow: (1) Flow, (2) Density, and (3)
These connections assist in planning, designing, and implementing highway facilities.
The terrestrial insects were the first organisms to take flight, carried by the air. Their ability to fly helped them eliminate enemies more easily and find food and companions more effectively.
Most insects benefit from the ability to fold their wings back and attach them to the body.
This provides them with a large range of small places to hide from wild animals.
Today, the only insects that cannot fold their wings backward are dragonflies or wasps.
Thousands of years ago, a man named Aristarchus said that the solar system revolves around the sun.
Some people thought they were correct, but many others believe the opposite—that the solar system revolves around the Earth, including the Sun (and other stars as well).
It seems sensible, because the Earth doesn't feel as if it's moving, does it?
The Amazon River is the second longest river in the world and the largest river by volume. It carries eight times more water than the second largest river.
The Amazon is not only the widest river on Earth's surface, but in some places, it is six miles wide.
Twenty percent of the water flowing from the world's rivers into the oceans comes from the Amazon.
The main River Amazons is 6,387 kilometers (3,980 miles) long. It collects water from thousands of smaller rivers.
