Theories of commodities are focused on what people want and what they like.
These theories suggest that people have specific needs and/or desires that arise internally as they move toward maturity.
These theories discuss what factors prompt certain individuals to engage in certain behaviors and what elements in their environment encourage or discourage them from performing or refraining from certain actions.
The expectancy theory of motivation and Herzberg's two-factor theory are two popular content theories.
Generally speaking, two attitudes may emerge among managers when leading their former colleagues. One aspect of this is that they are trying to remain "one of the boys (or girls)."
Managers of this type find it difficult to make unpopular decisions, carry out disciplinary actions, review performance, delegate responsibility, and hold people accountable.
In the other aspect of the image, a person transforms themselves into an unidentified individual who feels the need to completely alter and rebuild the work being carried out by the team.
Ultimately, the leader is finally accountable for the team's success or failure.
This behavior often creates rifts between leaders and the rest of the team.
Virtual teams adhere to the same standards of quality as renowned teams, but there are also subtle differences between them.
Virtual team members often perform the task of contacting their immediate physical group.
They have greater autonomy compared to traditional team members because their teams can meet across different time zones, which their local management may not understand.
The presence of a genuine "invisible team" (Larsen and Lofasto, 1989, p. 109) is also a distinctive feature of a virtual team.
The invisible team is the team to which every member is required to report. The invisible team sets standards for every individual member.
