Scientists are helped in their research by understanding the relationship between brain pathology and individual behavior.
It has long been known that various types of damage, trauma, injuries, and shocks reaching the brain affect human behavior and cause changes in certain mental activities.
The development of new technology enables us to observe and investigate brain structures and functions that were never observable before.
It provides us with a great deal of information and material to establish stimulation models, which helps us understand the processes in our mind.
Although AI is a prominent theme in science fiction, in reality, AI is a significant branch of computer science concerned with creating systems that can reason, handle behavior, learn, and acquire intelligence in machines.
Research in AI involves creating machines that automate tasks requiring intelligent behavior.
Examples include control, planning, and scheduling, client identification and ability to answer questions, as well as handwriting, voice, and facial recognition.
Such topics have become separate subjects focused on providing solutions to real-life problems.
Currently, AI systems are frequently used in fields such as economics, medicine, engineering, and military applications, including the development of numerous home computer and video game software applications.
A field trip is a major part of any classroom experience. Often, a teacher would like to take her students to places where travel by bus is not an option.
Technology provides solutions through virtual field trips. Students can view rare museum exhibits, tour aquariums, or appreciate beautiful artworks while sitting in their classrooms.
Participating in the virtual world's field trip is an excellent way to reflect on the experience and contribute insights for future classes.
For example, each year students from Bennett School in Northern Kiro Lina design a website for their journey to the state capital. Each year, the website is completely rebuilt, but older versions are kept online to serve as scrapbooks.
Blogs can also improve students' writing skills. Students begin their blogging with grammar and spelling mistakes, but eventually, the blog readership corrects these errors.
Since students are generally averse to criticism, bloggers therefore strive to improve their writing in order to avoid criticism.
Blogging also "compels students to become more familiar with their surrounding world." The need to engage readers (listeners) motivates students to be more alert and interesting (Toto, 2004).
Blogging is a tool that encourages collaboration and promotes students' skills in learning more effectively beyond traditional school days.
Appropriate use of blogs can help students become more analytical and critical, enabling them to actively engage with online content, position themselves in relation to others' writings, and outline their own perspectives on specific issues (Owoc, 2002).
