The foundation of the modern city of Casablanca was laid by the Berber Phoenicians in the 10th century BCE, and it was later used as a strategic port by the Phoenicians, Romans, and Vandals, who named it Anfa.
Portugal destroyed it and had it rebuilt under the name Casa Branca, leaving it only after the earthquake of 1755.
The Sultan of Morocco rebuilt the city under the name Dar al-Baida, and the Spanish merchants named it Casa Blanca, establishing their trading settlements here.
Casablanca Port is one of the least preferred places for shopping in Morocco.
Near the old city of Medina, you can easily find places selling traditional Moroccan items such as tagines, clayware, leather goods, hookahs, and various types of toys, but all these items are meant only for tourists.
Goma is a tourist city located in the extreme east of the Democratic Republic of the Congo, in Rwanda.
In 2002, Goma was devastated by lava from the Nyiragongo volcano, which engulfed most of the city's roads, particularly those in the city center.
Although Goma is reasonably safe, investigations are necessary to understand the ongoing fighting situation in North Kivu province, even for any travel outside Goma.
This city also serves as a base for climbing Mount Nyiragongo in the African region, where some fiery volcanic mountains are located.
You can use a boda boda (motorcycle taxi) to travel around Goma. The normal (local) fare for a short trip is 500 Congolese francs.
The term "Tumbuto," meaning inaccessible, was used as a metaphor for foreign, distant lands.
Today, Timbuktu is a poor region, although its reputation makes it a highly attractive tourist destination, and it has an airport.
In 1990, it was included in the list of World Heritage Sites in danger due to the threat posed by desertification.
One of the notable places to see during Henry Louis Gates' PBS Special on the African World was this.
This city is completely different from all other cities in the country, as it predominantly reflects an Arab temperament rather than an African one.
Kruger National Park (KNP) is located in northeastern South Africa, stretching from the border with Mozambique in the east, northward to Zimbabwe, and southward to the Limpopo River.
This park spans an area of 19,500 square kilometers and is divided into 14 distinct ecological zones, each of which is conducive to a different type of wildlife.
It is one of the most fundamental and attractive destinations in South Africa, and is considered one of the most important features of South African National Parks (SAN Parks).
Like all South African national parks, this park also has a daily conservation and entry fee.
It could also be beneficial for someone looking to purchase a wildcard ticket, which provides access to selected parks in South Africa or all of South Africa's national parks.
The region of Hong Kong is named after the island of Hong Kong, and it is the place that many tourists consider as their center for tourism.
The rows of buildings that form Hong Kong's skyline have taken on the shape of a glowing bar chart, clearly visible against the waters of Victoria Harbour.
Leave the island to get the best view of Hong Kong, and head to the waterfront opposite, Colombo Waterfront.
A large number of Hong Kong Island's urban constructions are concentrated on the available land along the northern coast.
This is the place that the British imperialists turned into their own, and similarly, if you're looking for evidence of the region's colonial past, this is a good place to start.
Sundarbans is the largest coastal mangrove belt in the world, stretching 80 kilometers (50 miles) from the coastal areas into the remote, underdeveloped regions of Bangladesh and India.
Sundarbans has been designated as a UNESCO World Heritage site. The portion of the forest located in the Indian region is known as Sundarbans National Park.
Forests are not just mangrove swamps—they also include a few remaining strips of magnificent forests that once covered the Gangetic plains.
Sundarbans covers an area of 3,850 km², one-third of which consists of water/mangrove swamp areas.
Since 1966, Sundarbans has been a wildlife sanctuary, and it is estimated that the area now hosts around 400 Bengal tigers and approximately 30,000 spotted deer.
Buses run throughout the day between bus stations located in different districts (across the river), although those specifically heading towards the east, Jaker, or Bumthang depart between 06:30 and 07:30.
Since buses running between districts are often full, it would be better to purchase tickets a few days in advance.
In most districts, small Japanese minibus buses are operated, which are comfortable and sturdy.
Shared taxis are faster and more comfortable for traveling to nearby locations, such as Paro (No. 150) and Phobjikha (No. 200).
The Oiapoque River Bridge is a cable-stayed bridge. It connects the cities of Saint George d'Oiapoque in Brazil and French Guiana across the Oiapoque River.
The height of these two towers reaches up to 83 meters, and they are 378 meters long, with both lanes being 3.50 meters wide.
The vertical clearance under the bridge is 15 meters. Its construction was completed in August 2011, but it remained closed to traffic until March 2017.
It has been decided to fully launch it from September 2017, when the Brazilian customs checkpoints are expected to be fully operational.
The Guarani were the most significant indigenous group, currently residing in eastern Paraguay as semi-nomadic hunters, supplementing their livelihood with agriculture as needed.
The Chico region was home to other local tribal groups such as the Guaycuru and Payagua, who sustained themselves through hunting, gathering, and fishing.
The 16th-century Pragoe, formerly known as the "Giant Province of the Indies," came into existence as a result of battles between the Spanish conquerors and local indigenous groups.
The Spaniards initiated the era of vassalage, which lasted for three centuries.
After the foundation of Asnawar in 1537, Paragai successfully maintained its local character and identity.
Argentina is famous for having some of the best polo teams and players in the world.
The year's largest tournament is held in December at the polo grounds located in Las Cañadas.
In other times of the year, one can also witness lower-tier tournaments and matches here.
For information on news related to the tournament and where to buy tickets for polo matches, check out the Asociación Argentina de Polo.
The official currency of the Falkland Islands is the Falkland Islands pound (FKP), which is pegged at par with the British pound (GBP).
Currency exchange is available only at the island's single bank, located in Stanley, in front of the F.I. C. West Store.
British pounds are generally accepted anywhere on the islands and also within Stanley credit cards, and US dollars are often accepted as well.
Credit cards from outer islands are likely not accepted, although British, European, and American currencies can be used; contact the owners in advance to confirm the acceptable payment methods.
It is almost impossible to exchange the currency of the Falkland Islands outside the islands, so please exchange your money before leaving the islands.
Since Montevideo lies south of the equator, it experiences summer when the northern hemisphere has winter, and vice versa.
Monte video is located in the lower latitudes; during the summer months, temperatures above 30 degrees Celsius are common.
Winter can be surprisingly cold: temperatures occasionally drop below the freezing point, but wind and humidity make the weather feel much colder than what the thermometer indicates.
There is no specific time designated for the "rainy" and "dry" seasons; the amount of rainfall remains fairly consistent throughout the year.
Although many animals in the park are accustomed to seeing people, wild animals remain wild and should neither be fed nor provoked.
According to park authorities, stay at least 100 yards/meters away from bears and wolves, and at least 25 yards/meters away from other wild animals!
Although they may appear very polite on the surface, arna, bharah, musk ox, bear, and nearly every large animal can attack.
Every year, dozens of visitors get injured because they do not maintain a safe distance. These animals are large, wild, and potentially dangerous, so give them space.
Also keep in mind that foul-smelling food attracts bears and other wild animals. Therefore, avoid bringing or cooking smelly food and keep your camp clean.
